.TH SPARKY-STEREO-HEADSET 1 "November 2026" "sparky-stereo-headset 0.2.0"
.SH NAME
sparky-stereo-headset \- the saved settings and the battery of a USB gaming headset
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sparky-stereo-headset status
[\-\-json]
.br
.B sparky-stereo-headset apply
.br
.B sparky-stereo-headset watch
.SH DESCRIPTION
The command behind the Headset page of System Settings and the Headset entry of the panel. It reads and sets headsets through
.BR headsetcontrol (1);
what a headset does not report is not offered. The choices a person makes are kept in
.I ~/.config/sparky-stereo-headsetrc
(KConfig), one group per headset, and only for the settings that were changed.
.TP
.B status
Say what is connected and what it reports; with \-\-json, the same as JSON. Exit status 1 when no headset is connected.
.TP
.B apply
Send the saved choices of every connected headset to it now.
.TP
.B watch
What the user service runs: the saved choices go to a headset when it appears.
.SH THE BATTERY
Plasma shows the battery of a headset in its Power and Battery applet, in the Info Centre and in PowerDevil's low-battery notice for peripherals, because they all read UPower. A headset for which the kernel has its own driver (hid-corsair-void, hid-steelseries-arctis, hid-logitech-hidpp) is listed by the kernel. For the others the system service
.B sparky-stereo-headset-battery
(/usr/libexec, started by udev when a listed headset appears and stopped when the last one is gone) reads the charge through
.BR headsetcontrol (1)
every 30 seconds and gives the kernel a virtual HID device through /dev/uhid whose only content is that charge. The entry goes at once when the headset does not say its charge. PowerDevil tells the person when the charge is at or below its level for peripherals (Power Management).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR headsetcontrol (1)
